<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phocaea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phocaea was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> city on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, near present-day Foça in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Its sailors were pioneers of Greek trade in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, founding Massalia (Marseille) around 600 BCE, which in turn founded Emporion. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> attacked around 545 BCE many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaeans">Phocaeans</a> fled west, and after the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> some founded Elea in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phocaea.jpg" alt="Phocaea" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: QuartierLatin1968 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_theatre_Fo%C3%A7a525.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
